import hashlib, json, re
INSTRUCTION = 'You design features for H&M recommendation: rank 105542 clothing articles for each customer at cutoff; predict purchases in the NEXT 7 days. Fitness is downstream LightGBM validation MAP@12 over the COMPLETE catalog, not classification accuracy. Generate ONE COMPLETE feature program as JSON. Use domain knowledge to discover useful nontrivial features, improve and combine previous successful programs. No pretrained embedding or human-engineered historical feature is provided.\n\nJSON format:\n{"rationale":"brief hypothesis","aggregates":[{"name":"f_unique_name","keys":["customer_id"],"sql":"DuckDB aggregate expression"}],"derived":[{"name":"f_another_name","sql":"scalar expression over raw and aggregate feature names"}]}\n\nThe host supplies a time-safe history relation; rows with transactions AFTER cutoff are physically excluded. Columns: price (normalized transaction price), sales_channel_id (1/2), days_ago (integer days since transaction), customer_age, product_code, product_type_no, graphical_appearance_no, colour_group_code, perceived_colour_value_id, perceived_colour_master_id, department_no, index_group_no, section_no, garment_group_no. These last columns describe the purchased article. Missing values can occur.\nAggregate keys can be [customer_id], [article_id], [ONE article category column], [customer_id,article_id], or [customer_id,ONE article category column]. The host computes GROUP BY and joins results to each candidate using matching keys. IDs are never numerical predictors: use them only as grouping keys or inside count()/count(DISTINCT ...)/approx_count_distinct() to measure frequency/diversity. Each SQL is an expression, not a query. FILTER (WHERE ...) is supported. Choose time windows yourself using days_ago. Functions allowed: count,sum,avg,min,max,stddev_pop,median,quantile_cont,approx_count_distinct,mode,coalesce,nullif,abs,sqrt,ln,log,power,greatest,least,round,floor,ceil,cast. For deterministic ties, use mode(column ORDER BY column).\nDerived scalar expressions may use aggregate feature names plus raw_u_age, raw_u_fn, raw_u_active, raw_u_club_member_status, raw_u_fashion_news_frequency and raw_i_ followed by one article column listed above. Derived features can refer to earlier derived entries. SQL CASE WHEN is allowed. Use NULLIF for division. No quoted strings, SELECT, FROM, subqueries, window functions, table access, target labels or timestamps. Do not invent columns. Do not use features from later data. The host fills remaining missing/inf with -1. Every program starts from original raw fields and must specify its complete chosen feature set, not additions to prior code. Keep JSON concise: normally 8-16 aggregates and 2-6 derived features, at most 24/12. Return only JSON, no markdown.\n'

def program_key(program):
    """Ignore explanation, feature names, group order and harmless SQL whitespace/case."""
    definitions = {}
    signatures = []
    for entry in program.get("aggregates", []) + program.get("derived", []):
        sql = entry["sql"].lower()
        for name in sorted(definitions, key=len, reverse=True):
            sql = re.sub(r"\b" + re.escape(name) + r"\b", "(" + definitions[name] + ")", sql)
        sql = re.sub(r"\s+", "", sql)
        signature = json.dumps([sorted(entry.get("keys", [])), sql], separators=(",", ":"))
        definitions[entry["name"]] = signature
        signatures.append(signature)
    return hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(sorted(set(signatures))).encode()).hexdigest()
